<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>

  <!-- D1: EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2 are off for the whole project.
       All 219 findings on 2026-11-01 were in designs where sharing a mutable object is deliberate:
       - 152 in JPA entities, plus 6 in MyUserPrincipal, ReminderService.ReminderResult and
         WeeklyPeerEvaluationReport, which wrap or report on entities. Hibernate tracks changes
         through the collection or association the entity holds, and services edit entities
         through their getters, so defensive copies would change behavior
         (see PeerEvaluation.getRatings / setRatings).
       - 27 in Spring bean constructors: controllers and services keep the beans Spring injects.
         That is dependency injection, not a leak.
       - 34 in DTO records: built per request by a converter, serialized to JSON, then discarded.
       Narrowing was considered and rejected: the code is packaged by feature, not by layer, so no
       package or name pattern separates these kinds of class.
       Revisit if Project Pulse adds value objects or a library API that outside code holds on to. -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
